    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok i see. so the seller just needs to provide proof of the policy at the discharge port. what happens if the goods are damaged before they reach that port but after they've left the seller? who is responsible for filing the claim then?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Under CIF conditions, since the seller's responsibility ends when the goods cross onto the vessel at the earliest shipping point, the buyer would theoretically be responsible for any damage that occurs before the goods are loaded onto the vessel. If the goods are lost at this stage, the buyer would typically need to file a claim with their insurers. However, if there are any issues arising during transit such as loss, shortage, or damage discovered in the goods, the cargo carrier (shipping company or freight forwarder) who onboarded the goods would generally be the party that submits the initial claim. The seller would need to inform the buyer and provide the details requested to help the buyer with the insurance claim process. Normally, the shipping documentation to which the buyer may need access includes the bill of lading and freight document, which provides details necessary for insurance claim procedures. If there's damage en route that falls within the buyer's responsibility (before the goods are loaded onto the vessel), then the buyer must promptly report the incident to their own insurance company, but they can do so only after they receive the shipping documents. This situation can be complex and might necessitate the involvement of legal advice to determine the proper course of action.",
      "source": "target",
